UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 3: Aural skills and listening', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down. A rhythm pattern is the mix of sounds and rests that rides on that pulse, so some beats carry a sound and some stay empty or carry two quick sounds. The beat can stay perfectly even while the rhythm changes. That is why they are different ideas, not two names for clapping. This lesson uses body percussion only: knee pats, claps, stamps, and finger clicks. No tracks and no instruments are required. Separating beat from rhythm lets you keep a group together while the pattern stays interesting.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nStamp an even four-count beat with one foot while you tap your shoulders on count 1, rest on count 2, tap once on count 3, and tap twice quickly on count 4. Which part is the steady beat, and which part is the rhythm pattern?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm. The stamps stay one sound per count. The shoulders do not: count 2 is a rest and count 4 has two taps. Doing both at once does not glue them into one idea. You can stop the shoulders and the beat still walks on.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAny clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse. That fails when the claps are uneven, because a beat does not stretch and shrink from count to count. Uneven claps are rhythm. The beat is the even grid underneath, which you may pat even when a clap is missing. If the sounds speed up and slow down, you have not found the beat yet.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nClick your fingers on every count of a slow four, and clap only on counts 1 and 4. Write one sentence naming the finger clicks as the beat and the two claps as the rhythm, and say what is silent on counts 2 and 3. Do not copy the knee-pat pattern or the shoulder-tap pattern. If clicking hurts, pat your chest for the beat instead and say so on the page.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Music","learning_intention":"A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down. A rhythm pattern is the mix of sounds and rests that rides on that pulse, so some beats carry a sound and some stay empty or carry two quick sounds. The beat can stay perfectly even while the rhythm changes. That is why they are different ideas, not two names for clapping. This lesson uses body percussion only: knee pats, claps, stamps, and finger clicks. No tracks and no instruments are required. Separating beat from rhythm lets you keep a group together while the pattern stays interesting.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MU4-LIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Click your fingers on every count of a slow four, and clap only on counts 1 and 4. Write one sentence naming the finger clicks as the beat and the two claps as the rhythm, and say what is silent on counts 2 and 3. Do not Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using beat on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse.. Correction: A strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse. That fails when the claps are uneven, because a beat does not stretch and shrink from count to count. Uneven claps are rhythm. The beat is the even grid underneath, which you may pat even when a clap is missing. If the sounds speed up and slow down, you have not found the beat yet. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating beat on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with beat, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about beat with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for beat. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for beat. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm","Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work"],"question":"Stamp an even four-count beat with one foot while you tap your shoulders on count 1, rest on count 2, tap once on count 3, and tap twice quickly on count 4. Which part is the steady beat, and which part is the rhythm pattern?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for beat; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of rhythm with clear evidence","Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse","A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down","A strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named rhythm, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name rhythm only, without linking it to beat","Apply beat to new material and name the rhythm used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse"],"question":"Which response best applies beat with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on beat. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse","A strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for beat","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Pat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse","Name rhythm only, with no linked example","Click your fingers on every count of a slow four, and clap only on counts 1 and 4","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies beat to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about beat with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating beat on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using beat on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for beat. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with beat, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse.. Correction: A strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-music-y9-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Music 7–10 (2024) outcome MU4-LIS-01. Content-point mapping is pending. Not NESA verified. No copyrighted recordings are required; examples are original descriptions or student-made sounds.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise beat.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use beat with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify rhythm and explain why it matters.","I can practise pulse independently on a new prompt.","I can notice rest needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat. For the rhythm, clap on count 1, leave count 2 silent, clap twice quickly during count 3, and leave count 4 silent. The knee pat never waits and never doubles, so it is the steady beat. The claps and the empty counts are the rhythm pattern because their durations are not all equal. Say \"beat\" for the pats and \"rhythm\" for the clap pattern before you try to do both together.\n\nTRY FIRST\nStamp an even four-count beat with one foot while you tap your shoulders on count 1, rest on count 2, tap once on count 3, and tap twice quickly on count 4. Which part is the steady beat, and which part is the rhythm pattern?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm. The stamps stay one sound per count. The shoulders do not: count 2 is a rest and count 4 has two taps. Doing both at once does not glue them into one idea. You can stop the shoulders and the beat still walks on.","explain":"A steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down. A rhythm pattern is the mix of sounds and rests that rides on that pulse, so some beats carry a sound and some stay empty or carry two quick sounds. The beat can stay perfectly even while the rhythm changes. That is why they are different ideas, not two names for clapping. This lesson uses body percussion only: knee pats, claps, stamps, and finger clicks. No tracks and no instruments are required. Separating beat from rhythm lets you keep a group together while the pattern stays interesting.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse. That fails when the claps are uneven, because a beat does not stretch and shrink from count to count. Uneven claps are rhythm. The beat is the even grid underneath, which you may pat even when a clap is missing. If the sounds speed up and slow down, you have not found the beat yet. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast an even walking beat with an uneven rhythm pattern.","caption":"The beat can stay even while the rhythm changes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Beat</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Even pats you could walk to</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rhythm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Long, short, and silent counts</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Pat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat. For the rhythm, clap on count 1, leave count 2 s Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Pat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat. For the rhythm, clap on count 1, leave count 2 s Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat. For the rhythm, clap on count 1, leave count 2 silent, clap twice quickly during count 3, and leave count 4 silent. The knee pat never waits and never doubles, so it is the steady beat. The claps and the empty counts are the rhythm pattern because their durations are not all equal. Say \"beat\" for the pats and \"rhythm\" for the clap pattern before you try to do both together. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a body-percussion sound with a silent rest.","caption":"Rests belong in the rhythm, not outside it.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sound</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">A body sound on a count</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rest</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">A count with no body sound</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAny clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse. That fails when the claps are uneven, because a beat does not stretch and shrink from count to count. Uneven claps are rhythm. The beat is the even grid underneath, which you may pat even when a clap is missing. If the sounds speed up and slow down, you have not found the beat yet. Here is a more helpful example: Pat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat. For the rhythm, clap on count 1, leave count 2 silent, clap twice quickly during count 3, and leave count 4 silent. The knee pat never waits and never doubles, so it is the steady beat. The claps and the empty counts are the rhythm pattern because their durations are not all equal. Say \"beat\" for the pats and \"rhythm\" for the clap pattern before you try to do both together.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer calls the even foot stamps the beat and the shoulder taps with their rest the rhythm. The stamps stay one sound per count. The shoulders do not: count 2 is a rest and count 4 has two taps. Doing both at once does not glue them into one idea. You can stop the shoulders and the beat still walks on.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Any clapping is the beat, so a pattern of long and short claps is the same thing as a steady pulse. That fails when the claps are uneven, because a beat does not stretch and shrink from count to count. Uneven claps are rhythm. The beat is the even grid underneath, which you may pat even when a clap is missing. If the sounds speed up and slow down, you have not found the beat yet. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA steady beat is the even pulse you could walk or pat without speeding up or slowing down. A rhythm pattern is the mix of sounds and rests that rides on that pulse, so some beats carry a sound and some stay empty or carry two quick sounds. The beat can stay perfectly even while the rhythm changes. That is why they are different ideas, not two names for clapping. This lesson uses body percussion only: knee pats, claps, stamps, and finger clicks. No tracks and no instruments are required. Separating beat from rhythm lets you keep a group together while the pattern stays interesting. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 3: Aural skills and listening","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Aural skills and listening","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nPat your knees on four even counts and keep that pat going as the beat. For the rhythm, clap on count 1, leave count 2 silent, clap twice quickly during count 3, and leave count 4 silent. The knee pat never waits and never doubles, so it is the steady beat. The claps and the empty counts are the rhythm pattern because their durations are not all equal. Say \"beat\" for the pats and \"rhythm\" for the clap pattern before you try to do both together.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast an even walking beat with an uneven rhythm pattern.","caption":"The beat can stay even while the rhythm changes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Beat</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Even pats you could walk to</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rhythm</text><text x=\"